{#-
  Aegis — insider-risk gate (spec 06 §4).

  Does not extend _base.yml.j2. Aegis runs no scanner and produces no
  findings, so the shared skeleton's upload step has nothing to carry: it
  reports *observations* about a pull request to /api/ingest/aegis and the
  platform scores them.

  Read spec 06 §9 before changing this. Unlike every other capability, what
  this workflow produces is personal data about a named colleague.

  Two properties this template is responsible for:

  1. **Nothing leaves the runner.** The scorer reads git history and the diff
     locally and posts only its conclusions — which globs matched, whether the
     author has contributed before. The diff itself is never transmitted. The
     one exception in the design is the AI-authorship classifier: it sends
     the diff to a configured gateway, and its step is rendered only when
     `ai_classifier_url` is set. Unset — the default — means no such step
     exists in the generated workflow, not that one exists and no-ops.

  2. **`permissions` is minimal and read-mostly.** spec 06 §6 requires that
     Aegis can never merge, close or force-push. `pull-requests` is
     deliberately absent: the recommendation is published as a Check Run, and
     write access to pull requests is exactly the capability that would let a
     bug close somebody's work.

  NOTE ON SHELL SYNTAX: no heredocs in these templates. Jinja's variable
  delimiter here is `<<`, so `cat <<JSON` parses as the start of a Jinja
  expression and the template fails to compile. Use `printf` or `jq -n`.
-#}
# Generated by Mykronos Workflow Installer — template version: << template_version >>
# Capability: aegis · Repo: << repo_full_name >>
#
# Do not edit by hand. A template resync will overwrite this file (spec 03 §6).
# To change thresholds or sensitive paths, edit this repo's aegis capability
# config in Mykronos — not this file.

name: Mykronos aegis

on:
  pull_request:
    types: [opened, synchronize, reopened]
  # Review integrity (spec 06 §2a) cannot be evaluated on `pull_request`
  # alone: that fires when the change is opened or pushed to, which is before
  # any review exists. Every one of those signals would see zero reviews
  # forever and stay silent — the capability would look like it worked.
  #
  # `dismissed` matters as much as `submitted`. An approval withdrawn after
  # the fact changes the answer, and leaving a stale "reviewed independently"
  # check run behind would be worse than never having posted one.
  pull_request_review:
    types: [submitted, dismissed]

permissions:
  contents: read
  # Publishing the recommendation as a Check Run is how a reviewer sees it.
  checks: write
  # Read, not write. spec 06 §6 requires that Aegis can never merge, close or
  # force-push, and all three need `pull-requests: write` — which stays
  # withheld. Reading reviews is what the §2a signals are computed from, and
  # `read` cannot mutate a pull request in any way.
  pull-requests: read

concurrency:
  group: mykronos-aegis-${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}
  cancel-in-progress: true

jobs:
  aegis:
    name: Insider risk
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    timeout-minutes: << config.timeout_minutes | default(15) >>

    steps:
      - name: Check out the code
        uses: actions/checkout@11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262  # v4.4.0
        with:
          # Full history: the author-baseline signal compares this change
          # against that author's own prior commits, which a shallow clone
          # does not contain. Without it the signal is skipped rather than
          # guessed (spec 06 §7), so the scan still works — it just sees less.
          fetch-depth: 0

      - name: Fail fast if Mykronos is unreachable
        run: |
          curl --fail --silent --show-error --max-time 20 \
            -H "Authorization: Bearer ${{ secrets.<< token_secret_name >> }}" \
            "<< ingestion_api_url >>/api/ingest/health" > /dev/null

      - name: Set up Python
        uses: actions/setup-python@a26af69be951a213d495a4c3e4e4022e16d87065  # v5.6.0
        with:
          python-version: "3.13"

      - name: Install the Mykronos signal collector
        # Same package the adapters ship in (spec 04 §4), for the same reason:
        # one definition of the submission schema.
        run: pip install --quiet '<< mykronos_package_spec >>'

{%- if config.ai_classifier_url | default(false) %}
      - name: Ask the configured AI-authorship classifier
        # spec 06 §5's one deliberate exception to "nothing leaves the runner
        # except conclusions", and the only setting in this platform that
        # sends repository content to a third party. It exists only because
        # this repository configured `ai_classifier_url`; with it unset this
        # step is not rendered at all, rather than rendered and skipped.
        #
        # Runs before the scorer, not after: its answer is an *input* to the
        # assessment. It feeds `ai_authorship_flag` and can raise the
        # `unverified_ai` signal that the pull-request description alone
        # would have missed.
        #
        # Every failure path ends at `ai_authorship_flag: null` — the state
        # the platform has modelled since spec 06 and never been able to
        # reach. A slow, down, or hedging classifier costs one flag and never
        # the assessment, which is why this step cannot fail the job.
        continue-on-error: true
        env:
          CLASSIFIER_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.<< config.ai_classifier_secret_name | default('MYKRONOS_AI_CLASSIFIER_TOKEN') >> }}
          # Attacker-controlled text, both of them. Written to files and
          # passed by path, never interpolated into a command line — the same
          # rule the signals step already follows for the description.
          PR_TITLE: ${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}
          PR_BODY: ${{ github.event.pull_request.body }}
          BASE_REF: ${{ github.event.pull_request.base.ref }}
          HEAD_SHA: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}
        run: |
          set -uo pipefail
          printf '%s' "$PR_TITLE" > classifier-title.txt
          printf '%s' "$PR_BODY" > classifier-body.txt

          # The diff, capped. An unbounded POST of a forty-megabyte refactor
          # to a third party is a different act from asking about a change
          # somebody could read, and a classifier cannot use what will not
          # fit in its context anyway.
          git fetch --quiet origin "$BASE_REF" || true
          git diff --unified=3 "FETCH_HEAD...$HEAD_SHA" 2>/dev/null \
            | head -c 200000 > classifier-diff.txt || true

          jq -n \
            --rawfile title classifier-title.txt \
            --rawfile body classifier-body.txt \
            --rawfile diff classifier-diff.txt \
            '{title: $title, description: $body, diff: $diff}' \
            > classifier-request.json

          # One retry, short timeout. The credential is a repository secret,
          # never embedded in this template (spec 12 §2). An unset secret
          # sends an empty bearer, the gateway refuses it, and that is the
          # null path again rather than a confident wrong answer.
          curl --silent --show-error --fail \
            --max-time 20 --retry 1 --retry-max-time 45 \
            -X POST "<< config.ai_classifier_url >>" \
            -H "Authorization: Bearer $CLASSIFIER_TOKEN" \
            -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
            -d @classifier-request.json > classifier.json \
            || rm -f classifier.json

          # The diff does not outlive the request. Nothing later in this job
          # needs it, and a copy left in the workspace is a copy something
          # else could pick up.
          rm -f classifier-diff.txt classifier-request.json \
                classifier-title.txt classifier-body.txt

          if [ -f classifier.json ]; then
            echo "Classifier answered. Only ai_authored and confidence are read;"
            echo "anything else in the response is discarded before scoring."
          else
            echo "::warning::The configured AI-authorship classifier did not answer. Recording ai_authorship_flag as null - not established, which is not the same as human-authored."
          fi
{%- endif %}

      - name: Collect signals
        id: signals
        env:
          # Written to a file rather than interpolated into a shell command:
          # a PR description is attacker-controlled text and must never be
          # able to reach a shell.
          PR_BODY: ${{ github.event.pull_request.body }}
          GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
          # Bound here rather than interpolated into the script below,
          # for the same reason PR_BODY is. `${{ }}` is substituted
          # before bash parses the line, so a value carrying shell
          # syntax becomes shell syntax. A login and a base branch are
          # narrow, but the rule is cheaper to keep than to re-reason
          # about per field.
          REPO: ${{ github.repository }}
          PR_NUMBER: ${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}
          HEAD_SHA: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}
          PR_AUTHOR: ${{ github.event.pull_request.user.login }}
          BASE_REF: ${{ github.event.pull_request.base.ref }}
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          printf '%s' "$PR_BODY" > pr-body.txt

          # Reviews submitted so far. Empty on `pull_request: opened`, which
          # is correct and not an error: the §2a signals stay silent rather
          # than reporting that nobody reviewed a change nobody has seen yet.
          #
          # `|| true` because a failed fetch should cost the review-integrity
          # signals, not the whole assessment — the sensitive-path and
          # baseline signals are still worth publishing without it.
          gh api \
            "repos/$REPO/pulls/$PR_NUMBER/reviews" \
            --paginate > reviews.json || echo '[]' > reviews.json

          # No `--author-role`, deliberately. The `privilege_adjacent` signal
          # wants the author's repository role, and reading it means
          # `/collaborators/{user}/permission`, which needs push access — a
          # permission spec 06 §6 withholds on purpose so that Aegis can never
          # merge, close or force-push. One signal is the right price for that
          # guarantee, and the scorer treats an unsupplied role as absent
          # rather than as ordinary access, so nothing is claimed here that
          # was not checked. The Concourse pipelines, whose credential already
          # carries push access for other reasons, do pass it.
          python -m mykronos.aegis_signals \
            --pr-number "$PR_NUMBER" \
            --commit-sha "$HEAD_SHA" \
            --author "$PR_AUTHOR" \
            --base-ref "origin/$BASE_REF" \
            --head-ref "$HEAD_SHA" \
            --pr-body-file pr-body.txt \
            --reviews-file reviews.json \
{%- if config.ai_classifier_url | default(false) %}
            --ai-classifier-file classifier.json \
{%- endif %}
{%- for pattern in config.sensitive_paths | default(['**/auth/**', '**/*secret*', '**/.github/workflows/**', '**/iam/**']) %}
            --sensitive-path '<< pattern >>' \
{%- endfor %}
            > signals.json

          echo "Signals observed:"
          jq -r '.signals[] | "  \(.key): \(.rationale)"' signals.json

      - name: Report to Mykronos
        id: report
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail

          curl --fail --silent --show-error --max-time 60 \
            -X POST "<< ingestion_api_url >>/api/ingest/aegis" \
            -H "Authorization: Bearer ${{ secrets.<< token_secret_name >> }}" \
            -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
            -d @signals.json > result.json

          SCORE=$(jq -r '.insider_risk_score' result.json)
          REC=$(jq -r '.recommendation' result.json)
          BLOCKING=$(jq -r '.blocking' result.json)
          CHECK_ERROR=$(jq -r '.check_run_error // empty' result.json)

          echo "Aegis: $REC at $SCORE/100"

          {
            echo "## Mykronos Aegis — $REC ($SCORE/100)"
            echo
            echo "A prompt to review this change carefully, not a judgement"
            echo "about the person who wrote it. See the check run for the"
            echo "specific observations."
          } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"

          if [ -n "$CHECK_ERROR" ]; then
            echo "::warning::Assessment recorded, but the check run could not be posted: $CHECK_ERROR"
          fi

          # Advisory unless this repo opted into blocking (spec 06 §4). Even
          # then the Check Run is what gates the merge; failing this step as
          # well would be a second red mark saying the same thing.
          if [ "$BLOCKING" = "true" ] && [ "$REC" = "block_recommended" ]; then
            echo "::error::Aegis recommends blocking and blocking is enabled for this repository. Human review is still required either way."
            exit 1
          fi
